Reason:
The absence of checks to prevent the addition of the same configuration type multiple times allows for duplicate entries during blueprint deployment. This can lead to conflicts and inconsistencies in the cluster configuration, particularly for service configurations other than `cluster-env`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
Collection<String> encounteredConfigTypes = new HashSet<String>();
if (encounteredConfigTypes.add(serviceConfigType)) {
  // skip handling of cluster-env here
  if (!serviceConfigType.equals("cluster-env")) {
    if (mapClusterConfigurations.containsKey(serviceConfigType)) {
      blueprintConfigRequest.addConfigElement(serviceConfigType,
        mapClusterConfigurations.get(serviceConfigType),
        mapClusterAttributes.get(serviceConfigType));
    }
  }
}
```
Reason:
The introduction of the `encounteredConfigTypes` set ensures that each configuration type is only processed once. By using `encounteredConfigTypes.add(serviceConfigType)`, the code effectively prevents duplicate configuration types from being added to the cluster, thus resolving the issue of potential conflicts and inconsistencies.

